<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Lotharingia</h1><p class="st-meta">903 CE – 959 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The duchy that held together the old middle kingdom of Lothair II, between France and Germany, before it was split into Upper and Lower Lorraine in 959.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lotharingia">Lotharingia</a> was the kingdom created for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-ii">Lothair II</a> in 855, stretching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a>. After the Carolingian lines weakened, the East Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-child">Louis the Child</a> appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-duke-of-lotharingia">Gebhard</a> as duke in 903. Local nobles, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reginarids">Reginarids</a>, repeatedly switched allegiance between East and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/west-francia">West Francia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The duchy held the old Carolingian heartland around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Liège</a>, rich in bishoprics and royal abbeys. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> went over to the West Frankish king before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-fowler">Henry the Fowler</a> recovered the region in 925; Gilbert drowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andernach">Andernach</a> in 939 while rebelling against Otto I. Otto's son-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-the-red">Conrad the Red</a> rebelled in 953.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Otto I then gave the duchy to his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruno-the-great">Bruno</a>, archbishop of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a>, who divided it in 959 into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-lorraine">Duchy of Upper Lorraine</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Duchy of Lower Lorraine</a>. Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a> became the later Duchy of Lorraine; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> principalities grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-lower-lorraine">Lower Lorraine</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-lotharingia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Lotharingia" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Furfur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vertrag_von_Pr%C3%BCm.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>903 CE – 959 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gilbert of Lotharingia, Conrad the Red, Bruno of Cologne</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French and Old High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a>, <a href="/countries/france">France</a>, <a href="/countries/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Lorraine (undivided), Lotharingia, Stem Duchy of Lorraine</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=931&amp;focus=duchy-of-lotharingia" aria-label="Duchy of Lotharingia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 416 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Lotharingia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5139 672L5139 672M5139 672L5054 728M5139 672L5199 691M5139 672L5143 733M5139 672L5139 672"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5139 672h0M5054 728h0M5199 691h0M5143 733h0M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5139 672h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5139 672h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=931&amp;focus=duchy-of-lotharingia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
